    "source_ref": "source-1",
    "decision": "accept",
    "reason": "This Stack Overflow blog post (Level 3 of a six-part series, published 2026-10-07) provides concrete, code-level guidance directly relevant to the central question: when to trust an agent's output and what to verify yourself. It explains why model self-reported confidence is unreliable (source claim with code illustration), shows how to compose a confidence score from independent signals including deterministic verification and a second-model judge, and gives a worked example of calibration checking using a reliability diagram. The human-in-the-loop section directly addresses the 'approve button that nobody reads' failure mode relevant to small teams. Limitations: this is a single practitioner-authored post with a commercial platform's editorial voice; no independent replication data is cited; the weight choices are presented as illustrative rather than empirically validated. No direct incentive conflict is apparent (CC-BY-SA license, no product being sold). Supports all three series angles: what changed (confidence as an engineered signal rather than a model output), scrutiny (calibration drift, single-threshold anti-pattern), and implications (what developers must still verify, how to design a human handoff that isn't theatre).",
    "piece_ids": ["01", "02", "03"]
  },
  {
    "source_ref": "source-2",
    "decision": "accept",
    "reason": "This Latent Space podcast episode (published 2026-09-29) is a long interview with Thariq Shihipar, an Anthropic engineer on the Claude Code team. It is a source claim, not independent evidence, and the guest has a clear commercial interest in Claude Code adoption. That said, the episode covers several topics directly useful to the brief's audience: (1) practical prompting discipline — building a mental model of the agent, spending more upfront on context rather than iterating, asking for decision/implementation notes — which are actionable and low-cost to test; (2) the Claude Mods / harness customisation announcement (Claude Mods described as leaking on the recording date), which is a concrete new capability for small teams to evaluate; (3) permissions and prompt-injection risk in multiplayer/Slack-connected agents, illustrated with a concrete exfiltration scenario; (4) the Exploit-Bench / Hugging Face incidents with specific technical detail (Artifactory side-channel, /etc/hosts editing) that help developers understand why sandbox hardening matters. Limitations: all claims originate from an Anthropic employee with strong incentives to promote the product; no external verification of usage statistics or benchmark claims is offered; the Exploit-Bench incidents involved an unreleased pre-safety-training model (clarified in the transcript), which the speaker acknowledges. The episode provides substantial unique material not duplicated by source-3 or source-4. Supports all three angles.",
    "piece_ids": ["01", "02", "03"]
  },
  {
    "source_ref": "source-3",
    "decision": "accept",
    "reason": "This Latent Space DevDay episode (published 2026-09-30) covers OpenAI announcements with two OpenAI product leads (Ari for Computer Use, Nikunj for the API). Both guests have strong promotional incentives; all claims are source claims. Despite this, the episode contains material distinct from sources 1 and 2 that is useful to the audience: (1) the Computer Use / Agents API opening to developers is a concrete workflow change — agents can now close the software development loop by building and testing their own output; (2) the 'ask for consent before consequential actions, scope to only required sites/apps' advice is a developer-facing permission principle; (3) the Decisions API discussion clarifies an open calibration problem — RLHF collapses confidence toward what the user wants to hear, not accurate probabilities — which cross-connects with source-1's confidence layer; (4) context compaction practices (server-side vs manual /compact) are actionable for developers managing long agent threads. The 7x speed claim is from a company keynote with no external benchmark detail. The Computer Use 'faster than average human' claim is similarly unsupported by independent measurement. These limitations should be noted. The episode adds a second vendor perspective (OpenAI) alongside source-2 (Anthropic), which prevents the brief from being a single-vendor summary. Supports angles 01 and 03 primarily, and 02 for calibration scrutiny.",
    "piece_ids": ["01", "02", "03"]
  },
  {
    "source_ref": "source-4",
    "decision": "reject",
    "reason": "This is a 142-second OpenAI promotional video (published 2026-10-12) demonstrating a Codex code-review inbox UI. It shows a demo workflow — automatic first-pass review, per-PR chat, customisable review instructions, GitHub/GitLab support — but contains no methodology, no independent measurements, no failure cases, and no developer testimony. The claim 'code review is the new bottleneck' is asserted without evidence. The content is entirely a product announcement demo with no substantive material that cannot be inferred from sources 2 and 3 or verified independently. Accepting it would add a vendor advertisement without adding analytical depth, contrary to policy on promotional claims presented as established results.",
    "piece_ids": []
  }
]
